namespace Eigen {

/** \page StructuredMatricesExamples StructuredMatrices examples

These programs solve problems from numerical PDEs, integral equations, signal and image processing, approximation
theory, statistics, control and quantum mechanics with the \ref StructuredMatrices_Module. Each one replaces a dense
or sparse factorization by an algorithm that exploits a structure of the problem -- a Kronecker product or sum,
Toeplitz, Hankel or circulant, Vandermonde, Cauchy, or diagonal plus low rank -- and checks its result against a known
solution or a general-purpose solver. The bracketed numbers cite the module's
\ref StructuredMatricesReferences "references".

\tableofcontents

\section StructuredExamplesGrids Separable operators on tensor-product grids

Finite differences on a tensor-product grid give Kronecker sums of 1-D matrices: with grid values stored with x as the
fast index, the 2-D Laplacian is \f$ D_y \oplus D_x = D_y \otimes I + I \otimes D_x \f$. \ref KroneckerSum applies
such operators without forming them, and \ref BartelsStewart solves them through the Schur forms or
eigendecompositions of the 1-D factors. LeVeque [1] covers the discretizations.

\subsection StructuredExamplePoisson2D The 2-D Poisson equation

The fast Poisson solver: two \f$ n \times n \f$ eigendecompositions instead of a factorization of the
\f$ n^2 \times n^2 \f$ matrix, with the second-order convergence of the discretization: the fast diagonalization
method of Lynch, Rice and Thomas [2].

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include StructuredPoisson2D.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude StructuredPoisson2D.out
</td></tr></table>

\subsection StructuredExampleHeat3D Implicit time stepping of the 3-D heat equation

The decompositions are computed once and reused at every step. A shift such as the identity of
\f$ I + \tau L \f$ goes into one factor, since \f$ I \otimes I \otimes I = I \f$.

