\subsection StructuredExampleADI Alternating-direction implicit time stepping

The Peaceman-Rachford ADI method [4] treats one direction at a time. Each half step is a \ref KroneckerOperator with an
\c Identity() factor, which costs nothing: the product is a sparse product with the 1-D matrix and the solve one
\c SparseLU of a tridiagonal matrix.

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include StructuredADI.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude StructuredADI.out
</td></tr></table>

\subsection StructuredExamplePreconditioner A non-separable potential

When a term breaks the separable structure, the separable part still helps twice: \ref KroneckerSparseView
assembles the sparse matrix in one expression, and the fast Poisson solver preconditions conjugate gradients.

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include StructuredPreconditioner.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude StructuredPreconditioner.out
</td></tr></table>

\subsection StructuredExampleTorusHelmholtz Periodic boundaries: the screened Poisson equation on a torus

With periodic boundaries the five-point stencil is a block circulant with circulant blocks, solved by \ref Bccb
through the 2-D FFT [5]. It is also the Kronecker sum of two 1-D periodic second-difference circulants, and the two
solvers agree.

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include StructuredTorusHelmholtz.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude StructuredTorusHelmholtz.out
</td></tr></table>

\subsection StructuredExampleSpectralDifferentiation Fourier spectral differentiation

On a periodic grid the spectral differentiation matrices are circulants [21], and their spectra are the wavenumbers:
\ref Circulant::eigenvalues returns \f$ ik \f$, \ref Circulant::rank counts the \f$ N - 2 \f$ nonzero ones (the
constant and the sawtooth mode are annihilated), and the pseudo-inverse \ref Circulant::solve integrates a periodic
function with zero mean. Derivatives and the periodic boundary value problem \f$ u'' - u = f \f$ converge
geometrically for smooth \f$ u \f$.

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include StructuredSpectralDifferentiation.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude StructuredSpectralDifferentiation.out
</td></tr></table>

\section StructuredExamplesSpectra Matrix equations and spectra

\subsection StructuredExampleLyapunov The Lyapunov equation of a mass-spring chain

\f$ A X + X A^T = C \f$ is \f$ (A \oplus A)\,\mathrm{vec}(X) = \mathrm{vec}(C) \f$, and the Sylvester equation
\f$ A X + X B = C \f$ is \f$ (B^T \oplus A)\,\mathrm{vec}(X) = \mathrm{vec}(C) \f$. This example computes the
controllability Gramian of a damped chain, whose system matrix has complex eigenvalue pairs; Simoncini [6] surveys the
methods for such matrix equations.

<table class="example">
