\section StructuredExamplesToeplitz Toeplitz, circulant and Hankel systems

\subsection StructuredExampleLoveEquation Love's integral equation and a preconditioned Toeplitz system

A translation-invariant kernel discretized on a uniform grid gives a \ref Toeplitz matrix. Love's equation [10] is solved
directly by \ref LookAheadLevinson [11] in \f$ O(n^2) \f$. For large systems, conjugate gradients use the FFT-based
Toeplitz product and Strang's \ref Circulant preconditioner [12], [13], \f$ O(n \log n) \f$ per iteration.

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include StructuredLoveEquation.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude StructuredLoveEquation.out
</td></tr></table>

\subsection StructuredExamplePade Pade approximation

The denominator of a Pade approximant [14] solves a \ref Hankel system in the Taylor coefficients, solved in
\f$ O(n^2) \f$ through its Toeplitz equivalent. Rescaling the variable equilibrates the coefficients and keeps the
system Hankel.

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include StructuredPade.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude StructuredPade.out
</td></tr></table>

\section StructuredExamplesSignals Signal and image processing

\subsection StructuredExampleDeconvolution Deconvolution by Toeplitz least squares

The full convolution of a signal with a kernel is a rectangular \ref Toeplitz matrix. \c LSMR [22] solves the
least-squares problem without forming it: products with the matrix and, through \ref Toeplitz::adjoint, its transpose
cost \f$ O(p \log p) \f$ each by circulant embedding [13]. The matrix-free solution agrees with the dense QR of the
materialized matrix.

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include StructuredDeconvolution.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude StructuredDeconvolution.out
</td></tr></table>

\subsection StructuredExampleDeblurring Image deblurring with periodic boundaries

A blur with periodic boundary conditions is a \ref Bccb matrix [5]. A motion blur annihilates whole frequency lines,
which \ref Bccb::rank counts exactly, so the pseudo-inverse \ref Bccb::solve cannot undo it and amplifies the noise
next to those lines. Tikhonov regularization stays within the class: \f$ A^H A + \lambda I \f$ is the Bccb generated by
the autocorrelation of the point spread function, computed with two products, and each \f$ \lambda \f$ costs one
\f$ O(N \log N) \f$ solve.

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include StructuredDeblurring.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude StructuredDeblurring.out
</td></tr></table>

\subsection StructuredExampleProny Prony's method

A sum of exponentials obeys a linear recurrence whose coefficients solve a \ref Hankel system in the samples [23]; the
roots of its characteristic polynomial are the damping rates and frequencies, and the amplitudes solve the dual
\ref Vandermonde system with complex nodes, which \ref BjorckPereyra handles in Leja order. The recurrence residual
over all samples is a rectangular Hankel product.

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include StructuredProny.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude StructuredProny.out
</td></tr></table>

\section StructuredExamplesApproximation Interpolation and approximation

\subsection StructuredExampleQuadrature Interpolatory quadrature

The weights of an interpolatory quadrature rule solve the transposed \ref Vandermonde system, the dual problem that
\ref BjorckPereyra [16] solves in \f$ O(n^2) \f$; on Chebyshev extreme points they are the Clenshaw-Curtis weights
[15]. The primal solve gives interpolation coefficients.

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include StructuredQuadrature.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude StructuredQuadrature.out
</td></tr></table>

\subsection StructuredExampleRationalApproximation A discretized Cauchy integral

Representing an analytic function by point charges outside its domain leads to a \ref Cauchy system, factored with
pivoting in \f$ O(n^2) \f$ by \ref CauchyLU [18]; the error decays geometrically, as for the trapezoidal rule on a
circle [17].

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include StructuredRationalApproximation.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude StructuredRationalApproximation.out
</td></tr></table>

\subsection StructuredExamplePolynomialFit Polynomial least squares and the Hilbert matrix

The normal equations of continuous least-squares approximation in the monomial basis have the Hilbert matrix, a
\ref Cauchy matrix [26]: \ref CauchyLU solves them in \f$ O(n^2) \f$ and \ref Cauchy::determinant gives
\f$ \det H_n \f$ in closed form where dense LU loses digits. The fitted polynomial is far less sensitive to
\f$ \kappa(H_n) \f$ than its coefficients, and matches the discrete fit by a dense QR of the rectangular
\ref Vandermonde matrix; Vandermonde products evaluate both.

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include StructuredPolynomialFit.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude StructuredPolynomialFit.out
</td></tr></table>

\subsection StructuredExampleSeparableLeastSquares Separable least squares on a grid

The design matrix of a tensor-product polynomial fit on a grid is the Kronecker product of two rectangular Vandermonde
matrices. \ref KroneckerOperator::leastSquaresSolve applies \f$ (A \otimes B)^+ = A^+ \otimes B^+ \f$ [27] with one
complete orthogonal decomposition per factor, and \ref KroneckerOperator::rank and
\ref KroneckerOperator::singularValues come from the factors too; the dense decomposition of the assembled matrix
agrees.

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include StructuredSeparableLeastSquares.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude StructuredSeparableLeastSquares.out
</td></tr></table>

\section StructuredExamplesStatistics Statistics and estimation

\subsection StructuredExampleGaussianProcess A Gaussian-process likelihood

A covariance that is noise plus a low-rank feature model [19], [20] is a \ref DiagonalPlusLowRank operator. Its solve
(Woodbury identity) and determinant (matrix determinant lemma) cost \f$ O(n k^2) \f$, the price of evaluating the
log-likelihood.

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include StructuredGaussianProcess.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude StructuredGaussianProcess.out
</td></tr></table>

\subsection StructuredExampleKalmanUpdate A Kalman measurement update

With a diagonal prior and \f$ k \f$ measurements, the posterior precision is diagonal plus rank \f$ k \f$, and
\ref DiagonalPlusLowRank::inverse returns the posterior covariance as another \ref DiagonalPlusLowRank, since the
Woodbury identity is the Kalman update [24]. The posterior mean is one \f$ O(nk) \f$ product and the posterior variances are
read off the diagonal; the dense \f$ n \times n \f$ computation agrees.

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include StructuredKalmanUpdate.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude StructuredKalmanUpdate.out
</td></tr></table>

